---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// V-701 — incident response + customer-facing policy docs.
---

<BaseLayout title="Incidents & SLA — Driftstack" description="What happens during a Driftstack incident: status page, severity ladder, customer comms, and SLA credit policy.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>Incidents &amp; SLA</h1>

    <p class="lead">
      This page covers what happens during a Driftstack outage: how
      we detect, communicate, and resolve incidents, and the credit
      policy if we miss our SLA.
    </p>

    <h2>Status page</h2>
    <p>
      <strong><a href="https://status.driftstack.dev">status.driftstack.dev</a></strong>
      is the single source of truth during an incident. It pulls
      from the same database the API uses to mark incidents, with
      a CF Pages fallback that serves a cached snapshot if the API
      itself is down (so the status page stays current even during
      our worst-case outages).
    </p>
    <p>
      Subscribe via the form on the page to get email updates the
      moment a new incident is filed or resolved. Slack /
      PagerDuty webhooks are available on Team Manual + above —
      configure under <code>GET /v1/account/me</code> →
      <code>incident_subscriptions</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Severity ladder</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Severity</th><th>Definition</th><th>First update</th><th>Update cadence</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Critical</strong></td>
          <td>Core API down across all customers, or data-loss risk.</td>
          <td>≤ 15 min</td>
          <td>Every 30 min until resolved.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Major</strong></td>
          <td>API degraded ({'>'}5% error rate) OR a critical surface
            (auth, sessions) unavailable for a subset of customers.</td>
          <td>≤ 30 min</td>
          <td>Every 60 min.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Minor</strong></td>
          <td>Single non-critical surface (recordings playback, GUI dashboard) degraded.</td>
          <td>≤ 60 min</td>
          <td>At resolution.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Maintenance</strong></td>
          <td>Planned change with potential impact. Always announced
            ≥48h in advance.</td>
          <td>Pre-announced</td>
          <td>Start + end.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Detection</h2>
    <p>
      Three signals trigger an incident:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>V-295b health probes:</strong> 60-second poller
        against <code>/v1/health</code> + per-region API endpoints.
        Three consecutive failures auto-create a Critical incident.</li>
      <li><strong>Customer reports:</strong> emails to
        <a href="mailto:support@driftstack.dev">support@driftstack.dev</a>
        and Slack channel monitoring. We acknowledge within 30 min
        during EU business hours.</li>
      <li><strong>Internal alerting:</strong> Sentry + cost-monitoring
        thresholds page on-call. Anything that warrants customer
        comms gets escalated to a public status-page entry.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Customer communications during an incident</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Status page entry filed</strong> with severity +
        title + affected components.</li>
      <li><strong>Email + webhook fan-out</strong> to subscribers.
        Webhook event type is <code>incident.created</code> with the
        same shape as <code>GET /v1/status/incidents</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Progress updates</strong> on the cadence above.
        Each update fires <code>incident.updated</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Resolution</strong> fires <code>incident.resolved</code>
        + a final email to subscribers carrying root-cause-summary
        + remediation steps.</li>
      <li><strong>Postmortem</strong> for Critical / Major incidents
        published within 7 business days at
        <a href="/legal/postmortems">/legal/postmortems</a>. Minor
        incidents get an inline summary on the resolved status entry.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>SLA + credit policy</h2>
    <p>
      Driftstack's SLA is on the <strong>API control plane</strong>
      (session create + lifecycle endpoints). Recordings, dashboard,
      and SDK distribution are best-effort.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Monthly SLA</th><th>Credit policy</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Trial Pack</td>
          <td>None</td>
          <td>N/A</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Solo Manual / API Starter</td>
          <td>99.5%</td>
          <td>5% credit on next month if missed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Team Manual / API Builder</td>
          <td>99.9%</td>
          <td>10% credit if 99.9% missed; 25% if 99.5% missed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Agency Manual / API Scale</td>
          <td>99.95%</td>
          <td>10% / 25% / 50% bands; auto-applied to invoice</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Enterprise</td>
          <td>99.99% (custom)</td>
          <td>Per-contract; can include refund + termination clauses</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Credits are <strong>auto-applied</strong> on the next billing
      cycle for Stripe customers. Crypto customers receive an
      equivalent tier credit (extra weeks on the current cycle)
      — we don't refund in crypto. To dispute the calculated uptime,
      email <a href="mailto:billing@driftstack.dev">billing@driftstack.dev</a>
      within 30 days of the missed-SLA month.
    </p>

    <h2>What doesn't count against the SLA</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Scheduled maintenance (announced ≥48h in advance).</li>
      <li>Customer-side issues (bad API key, exceeded tier
        concurrency cap, etc.) — those are 4xx responses, not
        downtime.</li>
      <li>Force majeure: regional ISP outages, upstream provider
        failures we can't route around (e.g. a Cloudflare region
        going dark).</li>
      <li>Issues caused by customer-provided integrations
        (NowPayments not reaching us due to your firewall rules).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Reading SLA data from the API</h2>
    <pre><code>{`GET /v1/status/sla
Authorization: Bearer ds_live_…

→ {
  "targets": [
    {
      "target": "api.driftstack.dev",
      "uptime_pct": 99.99,
      "total_probes": 43200,
      "fail_count": 4,
      "last_probe_at": "2026-05-11T13:00:00Z",
      "last_failure_at": "2026-05-09T14:23:00Z",
      "window_start": "2026-04-11T13:00:00Z",
      "window_end":   "2026-05-11T13:00:00Z"
    }
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Window default is 30 days; pass <code>?window_days=7</code>
      to widen / narrow. Per the
      <a href="/docs/audit-log">audit log docs</a>, every status-page
      mutation is recorded for compliance traceability.
    </p>

    <h2>Postmortems</h2>
    <p>
      Public postmortems for Critical + Major incidents live at
      <a href="/legal/postmortems">/legal/postmortems</a>. Each
      follows the same template: timeline, root cause, what we
      changed to prevent recurrence. Postmortems are blameless and
      detailed enough to be useful — we'd rather over-share than
      under-share.
    </p>

    <h2>Reporting a problem</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Acute outage:</strong> if the status page doesn't
        already show the incident, email
        <a href="mailto:urgent@driftstack.dev">urgent@driftstack.dev</a>.
        That goes straight to on-call.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-acute bug / weird behaviour:</strong>
        <a href="mailto:support@driftstack.dev">support@driftstack.dev</a>
        with a session id + timestamp.</li>
      <li><strong>Security:</strong>
        <a href="mailto:security@driftstack.dev">security@driftstack.dev</a>
        — PGP available on the page.</li>
    </ul>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
